### Case 1: $a = 1$, $b = p$ (prime)

Then $abc = p \cdot c$. For squarefreeness, $p \nmid c$ and $c$ is squarefree.

**Condition (b):** $bc = p \cdot c$ is a product of two primes. Since $abc = pc$ is squarefree, $pc$ is squarefree, so the two primes are **distinct**. This forces $c = q$, a single prime with $q \neq p$ (if $c$ were composite, $pc$ would have $\geq 3$ prime factors; if $c=1$, $pc=p$ has only one).

So we need **ordered pairs of distinct primes $(p, q)$ with $p \cdot q \leq 30$**.

Unordered pairs $\{p, q\}$ with $pq \leq 30$:
| Product | Pair |
|---------|------|
